  "markdown_report": "### Insights\n\n**Starting point.** (1) Sampled training margins measure discrimination against loader negatives. Their scale dependence prevents interpreting them as ranking quality. (2) Existing probes never perturb histories. They cannot establish whether recent or repeated evidence actually drives recommendations. (3) User feedback identifies unsupported specificity and possible redundancy. Histories alone cannot establish ownership, replacement intent, compatibility, or dislikes.\n\n**Related work.** (1) Oh and Cho [1] question excessive recency emphasis. Recent-history sensitivity therefore needs a matched control rather than an assumption that more recency is better. (2) Liu et al. [2] identify availability confounding in shuffled histories. Order interventions require temporal validity and careful interpretation. (3) Petrov et al. [3] report strong pairwise-transition baselines. Sequential accuracy therefore does not establish higher-order contextual reasoning.\n\n### Directions and framework\n\nThree directions follow: validate diagnostic measurements, distinguish history dependence from simple transition signals, and qualify unsupported semantic explanations.\n\n| Evidence layer | Existing signal | Proposed extension | Interpretation limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Representation | Embedding cosine | Retain as descriptive | Geometry is not ranking quality |\n| Discrimination | Training margins | Fixed-candidate rank changes | Candidate-dependent |\n| History dependence | Absent | Matched removals | Computational dependence |\n| Functional fit | Qualitative feedback | Supported/contradicted/unknown | Missing evidence stays unknown |\n\n### Candidate ideas\n\nScores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Matched history-removal probe — 6/8/3.** Originality: combines established interventions with category-balanced diagnostics, but introduces no new estimator. Potential: exposes reliance on recent evidence, but needs predictive validation. Difficulty: frozen inference is straightforward, but history reconstruction needs care. Related: [1,2].\n2. **Recency-weighted pairwise residual — 4/7/5.** Originality: combines SASRec and transition scores, but both components are established. Potential: tests whether simple transitions resolve failures, but may reinforce popularity. Difficulty: a sparse transition table is practical, but tuning and predictor synchronization add work. Related: [3].\n3. **Evidence-qualified explanation audit — 5/6/4.** Originality: transfers abstention to diagnostic claims, but uses familiar evidence schemas. Potential: reduces unsupported interpretations, but ranking gains are unproven. Difficulty: title-based evidence recording is feasible, but reliable compatibility judgments remain difficult. Related: [4].\n\n### Selected idea: matched history-removal probe\n\nAt 0% progress, prioritize a cheap, interpretable measurement improvement before changing architecture. On chronological pseudo-holdouts constructed exclusively from training histories, freeze each model and candidate set. Delete the latest nonterminal event and compare it with a uniformly sampled nonterminal deletion. Preserve the terminal event, delete equal counts, and rebuild both sequences identically.\n\n```text\nfor each eligible training-history holdout:\n    score baseline, recent-removal, random-removal\n    record paired target-rank and HR@10 changes\naggregate within category, then average four categories\n```\n\nRepeat controls with fixed seeds; bootstrap paired user outcomes within categories. Report sample counts and unavailable results explicitly. Preserve all diagnostic interfaces, restore model mode, and retain existing geometry/margin probes. These interventions measure model dependence, not user causality. Adopt model changes only through the unchanged pinned macro validation HR@10.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a matched history-removal diagnostic that compares removal of recent nonterminal evidence with equal-size random removal while preserving the terminal event and fixed ranking candidates.",
    "motivation": "The current probes measure embedding geometry and sampled training separation but cannot establish dependence on recent history. Early-stage research benefits from inexpensive measurements that can guide subsequent architecture and sampling changes without treating qualitative feedback as verified preference labels.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Access the actual network through view.model and use compatibility scoring hooks. Construct chronological auxiliary holdouts exclusively from user_train histories; do not change host splits. Use the same 99 unique auxiliary negatives across each user's interventions, excluding known training interactions and the target; mark users ineligible when insufficient candidates exist. Use the same truncation, left-padding, and position reconstruction for both deletions. Record counts, target-rank changes, HR@10 changes, paired differences, and metric definitions; return null with an explicit reason for unavailable measurements. Restore the network's prior training mode with try/finally. Aggregate within each of the four routed datasets and then equally across datasets. Bootstrap paired user outcomes within datasets, acknowledging that intervals exclude training-seed variation and adaptive selection. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], seeds and epoch defaults unchanged, and write artifacts beneath the trial directory. Run diagnostics on a bounded user subset to control GPU cost. No predictor changes are required for this diagnostic-only proposal.",
    "pseudocode": "save model.training; set eval mode\ntry, under no_grad:\n  for each routed dataset:\n    select eligible users deterministically from training histories\n    for each user:\n      target = final training event\n      prefix = preceding events, truncated to model context\n      require at least two nonterminal events\n      candidates = target + fixed unique auxiliary negatives\n      recent = delete latest nonterminal event from prefix\n      controls = delete one random nonterminal event per fixed-seed draw\n      score baseline and identically rebuilt intervention sequences\n      record target ranks and HR@10 indicators\n    summarize paired intervention differences and eligible counts\n  compute equally weighted four-dataset summaries\n  bootstrap paired user outcomes within datasets\nfinally:\n  restore original model mode\nreturn {metrics, metric_definitions}\n\ninterpreter prompt: include measured values, definitions, counts, missingness, and limits; prohibit preference or causal claims unsupported by evidence",
    "originality": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Integrates matched removals, terminal preservation, fixed candidates, and category-balanced reporting into the existing diagnostic contract.",
      "negative": "The intervention and bootstrap methods are established; novelty lies primarily in their task-specific integration."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a reusable basis for testing whether diagnostic changes predict ranking improvements across subsequent model variants.",
      "negative": "Sensitivity may reflect positional artifacts and does not itself establish useful recommendation behavior or improve HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Uses frozen-model inference and existing history and scoring interfaces without retraining or predictor changes.",
      "negative": "Correct reconstruction, auxiliary candidate sampling, eligibility handling, and paired uncertainty estimates require careful implementation."
    }
  },
